closure-rs
==========

closure-rs is a Rust port of the Closure Compiler (https://github.com/google/closure-compiler),
translated from its Java sources at release v20261006, commit
48f4107ca2aac52149546ccc42894522fcfdb17d (2026-10-06; npm google-closure-compiler 20261006.0.0).
It is an independent project and is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or published by Google.

Besides Closure Compiler itself, closure-rs translates parts of the Java libraries Closure Compiler
runs on, because the port reproduces their exact behaviour: OpenJDK 21 classes, Protocol Buffers for
Java, Guava, Gson and args4j. This file names every origin, its copyright holders, its license and
the closure-rs files that contain translations of it.

How the licensing is recorded
-----------------------------

Every Rust file under crates/ starts with the license notice(s) of the file(s) it was translated
from, copied verbatim, followed by "// Ported from ..." lines that name the upstream project,
version and files. Every ported function is marked with the method it corresponds to
("// port: Class#method"). A file with several origins carries each notice, and each part stays
under its own license. Files written for closure-rs without an upstream source carry the Apache-2.0
notice of The closure-rs Authors. scripts/license_headers.py regenerates the headers from the
markers; LICENSES/FILES.md lists every file that is not Apache-2.0 only, with its upstream files,
and LICENSES/headers.tsv records every file's header, licenses and upstream files.

License texts: LICENSE (Apache-2.0) and LICENSES/ (see LICENSES/README.md). The SPDX expression of
the whole is

  Apache-2.0 AND (MPL-1.1 OR GPL-2.0-or-later) AND GPL-2.0-only WITH Classpath-exception-2.0
  AND BSD-3-Clause AND MIT AND Unicode-DFS-2016 AND dtoa

Changes relative to the originals are the translation into Rust and the adjustments documented in
DECISIONS.md.

